@* The site wears the colours of whoever is winning.

   Everything gold on this site reads from three variables and a tint, so handing those four the
   leading team's colour repaints the whole thing — nav, buttons, headings, rules — without a
   single page knowing it happened.

   Written as a style block rather than a wrapper element on purpose: a div around the shell
   would be a new box in the middle of a layout that has a fixed topbar and a centred page, and
   custom properties inherit down the DOM whether or not there is anything to inherit through.

   Committee only. The standings are deliberately not shown to guests — see the note on the home
   page — and a site that turns red the moment Kyle goes ahead would tell them anyway.

   Which colour, and whether it is safe to write into a stylesheet at all, is AccentPalette's
   business; this only decides where it goes. *@

@inherits TripAwareComponent

@if (CanEdit && AccentPalette.Leader(Trip) is { } accent)
{
    <style>
        :root {
        --gold: @accent.Hex;
        --gold-rgb: @accent.Rgb;
        --gold-light: color-mix(in srgb, @accent.Hex 58%, #ffffff);
        --gold-dim: color-mix(in srgb, @accent.Hex 55%, #000000);
        }
    </style>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>Scores move it, and so does a team being recoloured out from under it.</summary>
    protected override TripArea Watching => TripArea.All;
}
